course: dictionary
lesson: 12607
title: Formal words: discourse
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Discourse is a formal word. You will meet it in serious writing, in news reports, and in church. As a noun it means serious talk or discussion between people. «There was a lively public discourse about the new library.» The word «public» is a clue: many people were talking and writing about one subject.
explain: Discourse can also mean a long, serious speech or piece of writing. «The minister's discourse lasted all afternoon.» The words «lasted all afternoon» tell you it was long, so this is a long, serious speech.
explain: Discourse can be a verb too, and then it means to talk or write at length about something. «He discoursed on the stars all evening.» The clue is «all evening»: he talked for a long time. Use the context to check your meaning, then swap it into the sentence to see if it still makes sense.
fact: Discourse means serious talk or discussion between people, or the way people talk and write about a subject.
fact: «Discourse» can mean a long, serious speech or piece of writing, such as a sermon.
fact: In church, Jesus' discourses in the Gospels are long teachings.
fact: As a verb, «discourse» is formal and means to talk or write at length about something.
fact: In language study, a discourse is any stretch of language longer than one sentence, such as a conversation or a connected text.
exercise: «The leaders held a serious discourse about the new road.» What does discourse mean here? | *Serious talk or discussion | A small snack | A quick nap | A loud shout
exercise: «The vicar's discourse lasted forty minutes.» What kind of discourse is this? | A game for the children | A cup of tea | *A long, serious sermon | A hymn sung quickly
exercise: «He discoursed on the stars all evening.» What does discoursed mean? | Forgot about | Drew a picture of | Looked up at | *Talked at length about
test: Which meaning fits discourse in «public discourse»? | A type of dance | Silent reading | Ordinary chat about the weather | *Serious talk and writing about a subject that many people share
test: In language study, what is a discourse? | A question mark | A short joke | *Any stretch of language longer than a sentence | A single word on a page
exam: In which sentence is discourse a noun meaning a serious speech? | Please discourse to the class quietly. | *The minister's discourse lasted all afternoon. | She will discourse about her dog tomorrow. | They discourse on art every Friday.
